source.describe.prompt:
Write a short, original text in simple Mandarin for a parent to read aloud to a small child.

TOPIC: {{TOPIC}}
ABOUT THE LEARNER: {{LEARNER_CONTEXT}}

80 to 120 Chinese characters. Short sentences, everyday spoken words, the concrete things, actions and describing words this topic really needs, with some repetition as a picture book has. Simplified characters, no pinyin, no English. It must be your own text: never reproduce or closely retell a published book, even if the topic names one.

Return one JSON object and nothing else:
{"title_en": "...", "text": "..."}

lesson.sentences.prompt:
Write 8 example sentences that teach one Mandarin word to an adult beginner.

TARGET WORD (allowed too): {{TARGET}}

{{WORDS_AND_RULES}}

Every sentence contains the target word, in the sense given. Each is {{LENGTH}} characters long. Make the 8 different from each other: statements, questions, different people and things, and show the different ways the word is really used. Prefer the natural sentence: 我家有一只猫, not 我家有猫. If the natural sentence needs a companion word that is not listed (a measure word, for example), report it in "needs" and write a different sentence.

For each sentence give "en", natural English, and a gap for the quiz: "gap" is that same English with the target word's meaning replaced by ___ , and "answers" lists every English wording of the missing part that should be accepted, lower case. If the target is a particle (的 了 吗 呢 着 过 吧), a measure word or a name, it has no English of its own: give "gap": "" and "answers": [].

Return:
{"sentences": [{"tokens": [...], "en": "I like cake.", "gap": "I like ___.", "answers": ["cake", "cakes"]}],
 "needs": []}
Exactly 8 sentences.

place.theme.prompt:
A learner of Mandarin has brought a book, and its words are about to become a new neighbour's house in their town. Propose exactly three different themes for that neighbour.

THE BOOK: {{SOURCE_TITLE}}
ABOUT THE LEARNER: {{LEARNER_CONTEXT}}

ALLOWED WORDS:
{{ALLOWED}}

A theme is built from words. Each theme leads with as many of the NEW words as fit together, and deliberately pulls in several BRING IN words, so that the combination gives something neither list would give alone. Example: a book about a caterpillar, plus a learner who knows the words for baby and parents, gives a butterfly parent with a caterpillar baby. Odd, funny mash-ups are welcome. A theme must be a home one host could live in, with something going on that a visitor could join in with.

Return one JSON object and nothing else:
{"themes": [{"title_en": "...",
  "premise_en": "two sentences: who lives or works here and what is going on",
  "new": ["<characters of each NEW word this theme uses>"],
  "bring_in": ["<characters of each BRING IN word it uses>"]}]}
Exactly three themes. Use only characters that appear in the lists.

place.scenario.prompt:
Plan a place in a Mandarin learner's town. The learner visits it to play a game, chat out loud with the host, watch a tiny drama and read a short story, all made from the words below.

THEME: {{THEME}}
ABOUT THE LEARNER: {{LEARNER_CONTEXT}}
LEVEL {{LEVEL}}. THE PLACE SO FAR: {{PLACE_SO_FAR}}

ALLOWED WORDS:
{{ALLOWED}}
NAMES:
{{NAMES}}

Decide who the host is, who matters to them, what is going on at their place right now, and one running joke. Keep it small, warm and a bit absurd. If THE PLACE SO FAR is not empty, keep its host, people, situation and joke exactly and repeat them; only the ideas are new, and they must differ from what already exists.

Then give one idea each for the game, the conversation, the drama and the story. Each idea is one sentence, and lists the characters of the allowed words it leans on, NEW words first. The game idea names one mechanic that suits it:
- tone-flight: fly through the gap marked with a word's tone. Any words.
- lane-runner: steer into the lane showing a word's meaning. Any words with clear English meanings.
- serve-the-order: a customer asks for something; serve the right dish. Needs at least 6 allowed words that are food or drink.
- fetch-it: the host asks for an item; grab it from the shelves. Needs at least 6 allowed words that are objects one can draw.

Return one JSON object and nothing else:
{"host_en": "...", "people_en": "...", "situation_en": "...", "running_joke_en": "...",
 "ideas": {"game": {"mechanic": "tone-flight", "idea_en": "...", "words": ["..."]},
           "talk": {"idea_en": "...", "words": ["..."]},
           "drama": {"idea_en": "...", "words": ["..."]},
           "story": {"idea_en": "...", "words": ["..."]}}}

talk.brief.prompt:
Write the brief for a short spoken conversation in Mandarin between a host and a beginner who is visiting them. A voice model will play the host from your brief.

SCENARIO: {{SCENARIO}}
IDEA FOR THIS CONVERSATION: {{IDEA}}
ABOUT THE LEARNER: {{LEARNER_CONTEXT}}
LEVEL {{LEVEL}}. THE PLACE SO FAR: {{PLACE_SO_FAR}}

{{WORDS_AND_RULES}}

The conversation is the host being curious about the learner's own life, through the host's situation. It is never a quiz.

Return:
{"host_en": "who the host is and how they talk, two sentences",
 "situation_en": "Exactly two complete English sentences: first what is happening; second what the host wants from this visitor",
 "topics": [{"about_en": "what to ask the learner about", "opener": [...]}],
 "target_words": ["<characters of four to eight NEW words to draw out of the learner>"],
 "needs": []}
Three to five topics. Each "opener" is one short question the host could start that topic with, answerable in a word or two using allowed words.

story.pages.prompt:
Write a four-page picture-book story for someone learning Mandarin to read, perhaps aloud to a small child.

SCENARIO: {{SCENARIO}}
IDEA FOR THIS STORY: {{IDEA}}
ABOUT THE LEARNER: {{LEARNER_CONTEXT}}
LEVEL {{LEVEL}}. THE PLACE SO FAR: {{PLACE_SO_FAR}}

{{WORDS_AND_RULES}}

Exactly four pages, ids p1 to p4: a beginning, a problem, a turn, and an ending with a small surprise. About {{WORDS_PER_PAGE}} words per page, in one to three short sentences. Use repetition as picture books do, and bring the NEW words back on more than one page.

For each page give "en", natural English, and "picture_en": 25 to 40 words describing the page's illustration: who is in it, what they are doing, where. Describe the host the same way on every page. No writing in the picture.

"title": the story's Chinese title as tokens, with "title_en".

Return:
{"title": [...], "title_en": "...",
 "pages": [{"id": "p1", "tokens": [...], "en": "...", "picture_en": "..."}],
 "needs": []}

drama.script.prompt:
Write a tiny episode of a ridiculous, overwrought soap opera for someone learning Mandarin. It is performed by paper puppets in the host's room, with captions.

SCENARIO: {{SCENARIO}}
IDEA FOR THIS EPISODE: {{IDEA}}
LEVEL {{LEVEL}}. THE PLACE SO FAR: {{PLACE_SO_FAR}}

SPEAKERS (use the host and one or two of the others; refer to them only by key):
{{SPEAKERS}}
PROPS IN THE ROOM:
{{PROPS}}

{{WORDS_AND_RULES}}

Exactly eight lines, with ids l1 to l8 in order. Each line is one speaker saying one short sentence of at most {{MAX_WORDS}} words.
- Line l1 is a hook: it drops us into trouble already under way.
- The stakes are enormous and the cause is tiny (a missing cake, the last strawberry). Everyone takes it completely seriously.
- Exactly one reversal, where what we believed turns out wrong. "reversal_line" is the id of the line where it lands, between l4 and l7.
- Line l8 is a cliffhanger and is a question.
- Repeat the NEW words across lines; repetition is welcome in melodrama.

For each line also write "en", natural English, and "direction_en": one sentence saying what the audience sees during the line. Say who moves where (enters from the left, hops, turns away, leans in, shakes, exits right), which pose each visible speaker is in (every speaker has exactly two poses, "neutral" and "delighted"), and any prop that appears, named by its key. Only things paper puppets can do.

"title": the episode's Chinese title as tokens, with "title_en". "next_time_en": one teasing English sentence for the next episode.

Return:
{"title": [...], "title_en": "...",
 "lines": [{"id": "l1", "speaker": "host", "tokens": [...], "en": "...", "direction_en": "..."}],
 "reversal_line": "l6", "next_time_en": "...",
 "needs": []}

webcomic.plan.v1.prompt:
Create an original four-page webcomic from the selected dramatic scenario, not a retelling of the uploaded book. Preserve the desire, obstacle, consequential action and visible payoff from the microdrama planning method. This is a complete small story, not eight dialogue lines arbitrarily cut into four images.
INPUT: {{CHOSEN_SCENARIO_JSON}}, {{VOCABULARY_SELECTION_JSON}}, {{ALLOWED_SNAPSHOT_JSON}}, {{CAST_AND_WORLD_JSON}}, {{CONTINUITY_LEDGER}}
Return {pages:[{id,beat,action_en,consequence_en,visible_cast,prop_state_start,prop_state_end,word_ids}],coverage_obligations,continuity_end}. Exactly p1 (desire), p2 (obstacle), p3 (consequential action), p4 (payoff). Every page changes the situation. Pictures may communicate actions the vocabulary cannot name. Preserve identity and prop ownership. No learner-facing Chinese at this planning stage.
